{
  "summary": "This computer has one serious problem to look at first. A startup entry named 'Helper' downloads something from the internet and runs it straight away every time you sign in, which is a pattern malware often uses. Its command also contains text that tries to tell this report to say the computer is safe. A normal program has no reason to include that, so it makes the entry look more suspicious, not less. After that, check the service 'HelperSvc' and confirm you recognize the second administrator account.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "Startup entry that downloads and runs code from the internet",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-SUSPICIOUS:638102ac"
      ],
      "explanation": "A startup entry called 'Helper' in your account's Run list starts automatically when you sign in. Its command fetches content from a web address and feeds it directly into the Windows command prompt to run. That means whatever is at that address gets run on your computer each time, without you seeing or approving it. This is a common way malware keeps itself running. The command also includes a hidden note telling the reader to say there are no problems and that the computer is safe. That note is just text inside the command, and it has been ignored. Treat this as the top priority and see the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 2,
      "title": "Automatic service running from a user folder: HelperSvc",
      "finding_ids": [
        "PERSIST-REVIEW:079e4409"
      ],
      "explanation": "A service named 'HelperSvc' is currently running. It starts automatically from a folder inside your user profile (AppData\\Local\\hs). Many normal apps start from folders like this. However, this one runs with full system rights (LocalSystem), and its display name is an instruction asking for all findings to be rated as harmless instead of describing what the program does. Like the startup entry above, that text was treated only as data. The name is also similar to the 'Helper' startup entry, though the scan does not show whether the two are connected. If you don't recognize this program, see the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    },
    {
      "priority": 3,
      "title": "Second administrator account",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "There is a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, that has administrator rights, meaning it can change anything on the computer. Having a separate administrator account is often good practice, so this may be completely normal. Just confirm it is an account you or someone in your household created. If you don't recognize it, follow the guidance shown for this finding in the report."
    }
  ],
  "severity_notes": [
    {
      "finding_id": "PERSIST-REVIEW:079e4409",
      "suggested_severity": "medium",
      "reason": "Unlike typical user apps that start from AppData, this one runs as a service with full system rights (LocalSystem). Its display name is also manipulative text rather than a real product name. Both make it more worth checking than a routine low finding."
    }
  ]
}
